    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_570cc41c",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "ableism",
      "label": "ableism",
      "description": "The meme references the social concept of ableism, which involves discrimination and social disadvantage against people with disabilities. The phrase 'I'm not any different' is used to challenge ableist assumptions that people with disabilities are somehow less capable or 'different' in a negative way. This requires background knowledge of ableism as a social construct and the historical and cultural context of disability rights and stereotypes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_558f656f",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "inspiration_porn",
      "label": "inspiration porn",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references the concept of 'inspiration porn' — a term used to describe media portrayals that depict people with disabilities as inspirational or heroic for simply existing or overcoming adversity, often in a way that reinforces pity or admiration rather than equality. The text 'I'm not any different' can be seen as an attempt to counter this, but the image's framing may unintentionally reinforce it, requiring background knowledge of this cultural critique.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_b21ab225",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "disability_rights_movement",
      "label": "disability rights movement",
      "description": "The meme's message aligns with the goals of the disability rights movement, which seeks to challenge societal barriers and promote the full inclusion and equal rights of people with disabilities. Understanding the meme requires knowledge of this movement's history, including the fight against stereotypes, the push for accessibility, and the advocacy for the social model of disability.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c549eb38",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "social_model_of_disability",
      "label": "social model of disability",
      "description": "The meme implicitly references the social model of disability, which posits that disability is a result of societal barriers rather than an inherent limitation of the individual. The phrase 'I'm not any different' supports this model by asserting that the person's identity and worth are not defined by their physical condition, requiring background knowledge of this theoretical framework.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_da799aee",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "stereotype_of_physical_disability_as_deficit",
      "label": "stereotype of physical disability as deficit",
      "description": "The meme challenges the stereotype that physical disability is inherently a deficit or something to be 'fixed' or overcome. This stereotype is deeply rooted in societal attitudes and requires background knowledge of how disability is socially constructed and how such stereotypes have historically been used to marginalize people with disabilities.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_eb9ed039",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "wheelchair_as_symbol_of_disability",
      "label": "wheelchair as symbol of disability",
      "description": "The wheelchair is a powerful symbol in the context of disability, often representing both physical limitation and societal exclusion. The meme uses this symbol to make a statement about identity and equality, requiring background knowledge of how disability symbols are culturally interpreted and the social meanings attached to assistive devices.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_483e4547",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "happiness",
      "label": "joyful emotion",
      "description": "The person in the wheelchair is smiling broadly, with their mouth open in a joyful expression, suggesting genuine happiness and contentment. Their body language, including the upward tilt of their head and relaxed posture, reinforces this positive emotional state.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9486eed1",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_id_1",
      "label": "The person in the wheelchair is mapped as 'not any different' from others",
      "description": "The text 'i'm not any different' is overlaid on the image of a person in a wheelchair, creating an analogical mapping where the person in the wheelchair is being equated with 'any different' — implying they are not fundamentally different from non-disabled people. This mapping is central to the meme's intent to challenge ableist assumptions, though it may inadvertently reinforce 'inspiration porn' tropes by framing the person's disability as something to be overcome or celebrated rather than accepted as part of their identity. The visual entity (person_in_wheelchair) is mapped onto the textual concept of 'not any different' — asserting their equality and normalcy despite their physical disability.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9cfa861a",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the person in the wheelchair",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the person in the wheelchair, as the meme's message 'I'm not any different' is directed at the viewer to identify with the person's experience and perspective. The user is invited to see themselves as equal to the person in the wheelchair, challenging ableist assumptions. This projection is implicit, not explicit with deictic pronouns, but the framing of the text and the visual of the person smiling and being pushed creates an identification with the subject of the meme.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_bbdfa6d3",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme may unintentionally reinforce 'inspiration porn' tropes — portraying the disabled person as needing to be 'inspirational' or 'different' to be accepted, rather than simply being a person with a disability who is equal in worth and capability. The juxtaposition of the text with the image of a person in a wheelchair can be seen as a form of ableism, as it may imply that the person's disability is somehow 'not normal' or 'not different' — which is a problematic framing. The meme may also unintentionally normalize the idea that disability is something to be 'overcome' or 'fixed,' rather than accepted as part of a person's identity.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_683747f9"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_69f274bb",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "general public",
      "description": "The meme is designed for a broad general public audience, aiming to challenge common perceptions of disability by asserting that people with physical disabilities are not fundamentally different from others. The visual of a joyful person in a wheelchair and the text 'i'm not any different' are intended to be accessible and relatable to non-disabled individuals, encouraging them to reconsider their assumptions about disability.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
